# AmicaleStar - Design Patterns & Visual Examples

## Design Pattern Reference

### 1. Model-View-Controller (MVC) - Angular Implementation

#### Without Best Practices (❌ Tightly Coupled)
```typescript
// BAD: Logic mixed in component
@Component({
  selector: 'app-bad-inscription',
  template: `<div>{{ result }}</div>`
})
export class BadInscriptionComponent {
  result: string;
  
  constructor(private http: HttpClient) {}
  
  register() {
    // HTTP call directly in component
    this.http.post('/api/inscriptions', data).subscribe(
      response => {
        this.result = response.message;
        // Validation here
        if (response.email) {
          // Save to localStorage directly
          localStorage.setItem('userData', JSON.stringify(response));
        }
      }
    );
  }
}
```

**Problems**:
- ❌ Hard to test (depends on HttpClient)
- ❌ Replication of logic across components
- ❌ Mixing concerns
- ❌ Difficult to change API

#### With Best Practices (✅ Clean Separation)
```typescript
// Service Layer - Handles all API logic
@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class InscriptionService {
  constructor(private http: HttpClient) {}
  
  register(data: InscriptionData): Observable<Registration> {
    return this.http.post<Registration>('/api/inscriptions', data);
  }
}

// Component Layer - Handles UI only
@Component({
  selector: 'app-inscription',
  imports: [ReactiveFormsModule],
  template: `<div>{{ result }}</div>`
})
export class InscriptionComponent {
  result: string;
  
  constructor(private service: InscriptionService) {}
  
  register() {
    this.service.register(data).subscribe(response => {
      this.result = response.message;
    });
  }
}
```

**Benefits**:
- ✅ Easy to test (mock service)
- ✅ Reusable across components
- ✅ Clear separation of concerns
- ✅ Single Responsibility Principle

