### 3. Decorator Pattern - Spring Security

#### Without Decorators (❌ Scattered Authorization)
```java
// BAD: Authorization checks everywhere
@RestController
public class InscriptionController {
  
  @PostMapping("/api/inscriptions")
  public ResponseEntity<Inscription> create(
      @RequestBody InscriptionDTO dto,
      HttpServletRequest request) {
    
    // Manual authorization check
    String token = request.getHeader("Authorization");
    if (!validateToken(token)) {
      throw new UnauthorizedException();
    }
    
    User user = extractUserFromToken(token);
    if (!user.hasRole("BUREAU")) {
      throw new ForbiddenException();
    }
    
    // Finally, actual business logic
    return service.create(dto);
  }
}
```

#### With Decorators (✅ Declarative)
```java
// GOOD: Decorators handle authorization
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/inscriptions")
public class InscriptionController {
  
  @PostMapping
  @PreAuthorize("hasRole('BUREAU')")  // ← Declarative!
  public ResponseEntity<Inscription> create(
      @Valid @RequestBody InscriptionDTO dto,
      @AuthenticationPrincipal UserDetails user) {
    
    // Focus on business logic
    return service.create(dto);
  }
  
  @GetMapping("/{id}")
  @PreAuthorize("hasRole('BUREAU') or @inscriptionService.isOwner(#id, #user)")
  public ResponseEntity<Inscription> getById(
      @PathVariable Long id,
      @AuthenticationPrincipal UserDetails user) {
    
    return ResponseEntity.ok(service.findById(id));
  }
}
```

**Benefits**:
- ✅ Cleaner code
- ✅ Consistent authorization
- ✅ Easier to understand intent
- ✅ Less boilerplate

