### 5. Dependency Injection Pattern

#### Without DI (❌ Tight Coupling)
```java
// BAD: Creating dependencies directly
public class InscriptionService {
  private EmailService emailService;
  private NotificationService notificationService;
  private SmsService smsService;
  
  public InscriptionService() {
    this.emailService = new EmailService();
    this.notificationService = new NotificationService();
    this.smsService = new SmsService();
  }
  
  public void register(User user) {
    // If EmailService changes, must modify here
    emailService.send(user.getEmail(), "Welcome!");
  }
}
```

**Problems**:
- ❌ Hard to test (real services created)
- ❌ Can't swap implementations
- ❌ Tight coupling
- ❌ Hard to change dependencies

#### With DI (✅ Loose Coupling)
```java
// GOOD: Dependencies injected
@Service
public class InscriptionService {
  private final EmailService emailService;
  private final NotificationService notificationService;
  
  // Constructor injection
  @Autowired
  public InscriptionService(
      EmailService emailService,
      NotificationService notificationService) {
    this.emailService = emailService;
    this.notificationService = notificationService;
  }
  
  public void register(User user) {
    emailService.send(user.getEmail(), "Welcome!");
  }
}

// In test - Easy to mock
@SpringBootTest
public class InscriptionServiceTest {
  
  @Test
  public void testRegister() {
    // Mock dependencies
    EmailService mockEmail = mock(EmailService.class);
    NotificationService mockNotif = mock(NotificationService.class);
    
    // Inject mocks
    InscriptionService service = 
      new InscriptionService(mockEmail, mockNotif);
    
    // Test without real services
    service.register(new User());
    verify(mockEmail).send(...);
  }
}
```

**Benefits**:
- ✅ Easy to test
- ✅ Can swap implementations
- ✅ Loose coupling
- ✅ Better code organization

